    <h2 style="margin-top:0">How the credit works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Month one:</strong> $175, the standard rate. The first payment starts the build, as it does for every client.</li>
      <li><strong>Month two:</strong> $0. The credit is applied to your second invoice automatically, which reads "credit applied" and totals $0.</li>
      <li><strong>Month three onward:</strong> back to $175, with the same plan, the same scope, and the same direct support.</li>
      <li><strong>Total saving:</strong> $175. The rate never changes, and there's nothing to keep track of.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;background:var(--bg-alt);border-left:3px solid var(--gold-500);border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.65">I give a free month instead of a small discount on every bill for two reasons. One credit on one invoice is simple for both of us. And a whole month free feels like a gift, while a few dollars off each month disappears into the bill.</p>

    <h2>How to claim it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Tell me which credit you're claiming.</strong> Mention it when you sign up, or in your message if you'd like to <a href="/contact/" class="inline-link">book a free call</a> first.</li>
      <li><strong>Email me one photo</strong> of a qualifying document from the lists below. It takes about 30 seconds. I delete the file as soon as I've checked it, and I keep no copies.</li>
      <li><strong>I add the credit to your account.</strong> Your first invoice is $175, your second is $0, and from the third month on you pay the standard $175.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="veterans">Veterans and military families</h2>
    <p>Anyone with U.S. or Canadian military service qualifies, along with their immediate family:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Active duty</strong> in any branch: Army, Navy, Marines, Air Force, Space Force, Coast Guard, U.S. Public Health Service, NOAA Corps, or the Canadian Armed Forces.</li>
      <li><strong>Reserves and National Guard</strong>, including Canada's Reserve Force.</li>
      <li><strong>Veterans</strong> with an honorable discharge, whatever the length of service.</li>
      <li><strong>Retired military</strong> and <strong>service-connected disabled veterans</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Family:</strong> spouses, children, parents, and siblings of anyone above, including Gold Star families and surviving spouses.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>To verify,</strong> send one photo or scan of any of these: a DD-214, a Veteran ID Card, a VA ID, a military ID (with sensitive numbers covered), a state driver's license marked "Veteran", or Canadian Armed Forces or Veterans Affairs Canada papers. Family members send the service member's document with a one-line note saying how you're related.</p>
    <p>My family and several close friends are veterans, and this credit is a small way to say thank you. Many owners in the trades I serve came to them after their service, and for some, a free month is what makes it easy to give a new website a try.</p>

    <h2 id="first-responders">First responders</h2>
    <p>Anyone serving now or retired from one of these roles qualifies, along with their immediate family:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Law enforcement:</strong> sworn officers at the municipal, county, state, or federal level, including corrections officers, parole officers, and sheriff's deputies.</li>
      <li><strong>Firefighters:</strong> career, volunteer, wildland, and structural, including federal and state wildland crews and rural fire-district volunteers.</li>
      <li><strong>EMS:</strong> EMTs, paramedics, advanced and flight paramedics, and ambulance crews.</li>
      <li><strong>911 dispatchers</strong> and call-takers.</li>
      <li><strong>Search and rescue</strong> members, paid or volunteer, with a recognized organization.</li>
      <li><strong>Canadian services:</strong> the RCMP, provincial and territorial police (including the OPP and the S&ucirc;ret&eacute; du Qu&eacute;bec), and Canadian fire and EMS services.</li>
      <li><strong>Family:</strong> spouses, children, parents, and siblings of anyone above, including the surviving spouses of those who died in the line of duty.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>To verify,</strong> send one photo of your department or agency ID, a badge that shows your department's name, or a recent paystub from a qualifying agency (with the amounts covered). Volunteers can send a letter from their chief or commander on department letterhead. Retirees can send a retiree ID or their retirement papers. Family members send the responder's document with a one-line note saying how you're related.</p>
    <p>Many first responders run a business on the side or after they retire: contracting, security consulting, training, fire watch, building inspections, and more. Their schedules are hard, and the work is harder than most people realize. A free month makes it easier to try a new website without the risk.</p>

    <h2 id="medical">Licensed medical professionals</h2>
    <p>Any licensed professional in active practice or in licensed retirement qualifies, along with their immediate family. That includes anyone building a private practice or a side practice:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Physicians:</strong> MDs, DOs, residents, and fellows.</li>
      <li><strong>Dental:</strong> dentists (DDS and DMD), hygienists, oral surgeons, orthodontists, endodontists, periodontists, and prosthodontists.</li>
      <li><strong>Nursing:</strong> RNs, LPNs and LVNs, nurse practitioners, CRNAs, midwives, and nursing assistants.</li>
      <li><strong>Allied health:</strong> physician assistants, physical, occupational, and respiratory therapists, speech-language pathologists, audiologists, optometrists, opticians, podiatrists, chiropractors, registered dietitians, and pharmacists.</li>
      <li><strong>Mental health:</strong> licensed psychologists, counselors and therapists (LCSW, LMFT, LPC, and LMHC), board-certified psychiatrists, and board-certified behavior analysts.</li>
      <li><strong>Imaging and lab:</strong> radiologic technologists, sonographers, medical lab scientists, and state-licensed phlebotomists.</li>
      <li><strong>Family:</strong> spouses, children, parents, and siblings of anyone above.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>To verify,</strong> send one photo of your active license, your state board card, a hospital ID that shows your clinical role, or a current verification printout from your state licensing board. Family members send the clinician's license with a one-line note saying how you're related.</p>
    <p><strong>A note on patient privacy.</strong> I build your practice's website: the pages, the service descriptions, the hours, and the location. I don't store or handle patient health information on it. Intake forms, scheduling, and anything else that collects health details go through a HIPAA-compliant provider such as Jotform HIPAA, Tebra, SimplePractice, or Healee, depending on your specialty. Your site links to or embeds their forms, and the data never passes through my servers. We'll set this up together during the build.</p>
    <p>More clinicians are opening independent or part-time practices. Licensing and credentialing take a great deal of work, the hours are long, and the stakes are real. A free month makes it easier to try a new website without the risk.</p>

  </div>
